1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,557
Watch closely.

2
00:00:01,907 --> 00:00:06,017
Every time this single photon detector
fires, it goes blind.

3
00:00:06,367 --> 00:00:10,779
And every photon that arrives during that
blind window is gone forever.

4
00:00:11,279 --> 00:00:14,650
This is Mars, the Markov renewal single
photon lidar

5
00:00:14,650 --> 00:00:18,280
simulator, presented at E C C V two
thousand twenty six.

6
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:21,750
A single photon lidar fires a pulse and
records

7
00:00:21,750 --> 00:00:24,910
the arrival times of individual returning
photons.

8
00:00:25,260 --> 00:00:27,716
Over many cycles each pixel builds a
timing

9
00:00:27,716 --> 00:00:30,972
histogram, and the array builds a three
dimensional cube.

10
00:00:31,322 --> 00:00:35,200
That cube feeds every learned depth and
reflectivity pipeline.

11
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:37,639
So how do you simulate that cube?

12
00:00:37,989 --> 00:00:43,538
A Poisson model is fast, but it ignores
dead time, so the physics is simply wrong.

13
00:00:43,888 --> 00:00:46,956
The sequential gold standard is correct,
but it

14
00:00:46,956 --> 00:00:50,808
examines every photon one at a time, and
it does not scale.

15
00:00:51,158 --> 00:00:53,329
Until now, you had to pick one.

16
00:00:53,829 --> 00:00:57,753
Our key idea is to factor the histogram
into two parts.

17
00:00:58,103 --> 00:01:02,039
A count: how many photons the detector
actually registers.

18
00:01:02,389 --> 00:01:04,885
And a shape: where in time they land.

19
00:01:05,235 --> 00:01:07,778
Earlier work already solved the shape.

20
00:01:08,128 --> 00:01:10,635
The count under dead time was an open
problem.

21
00:01:10,985 --> 00:01:12,483
Mars solves the count.

22
00:01:12,833 --> 00:01:15,527
That one change makes everything else
possible.

23
00:01:16,027 --> 00:01:17,652
Here is why the count is hard.

24
00:01:18,002 --> 00:01:22,762
Dead time couples each detection to the
next, so classical tools break down.

25
00:01:23,112 --> 00:01:25,350
Detections are not independent, and the

26
00:01:25,350 --> 00:01:27,989
waiting times are not identically
distributed.

27
00:01:28,339 --> 00:01:31,682
So we model registration as a Markov
renewal process.

28
00:01:32,032 --> 00:01:37,617
An embedded Markov chain says where the
next photon lands; a holding time says
when.

29
00:01:38,117 --> 00:01:40,044
And here is the key insight.

30
00:01:40,394 --> 00:01:42,414
Counting is the inverse of waiting.

31
00:01:42,764 --> 00:01:45,012
If you know how long you typically wait

32
00:01:45,012 --> 00:01:47,664
between detections, you know how many you
get.

33
00:01:48,014 --> 00:01:50,306
The embedded chain mixes quickly, so the

34
00:01:50,306 --> 00:01:53,401
cumulative waiting time obeys a central
limit theorem.

35
00:01:53,751 --> 00:01:56,607
Inverting that relationship gives the
photon count.

36
00:01:56,957 --> 00:01:59,777
This is the first closed form count
distribution

37
00:01:59,777 --> 00:02:02,715
under dead time, with no per photon loop
anywhere.

38
00:02:03,215 --> 00:02:05,049
But the variance hides a subtlety.

39
00:02:05,399 --> 00:02:08,980
It is an infinite series of covariance
terms, and depending

40
00:02:08,980 --> 00:02:12,621
on flux and dead time, they decay at
wildly different rates.

41
00:02:12,971 --> 00:02:15,711
A fixed cutoff is either biased or
expensive.

42
00:02:16,061 --> 00:02:20,531
Instead we sum the tail in closed form
using the five dominant eigenmodes.

43
00:02:20,881 --> 00:02:23,934
Twelve times lower error, at lower cost.

44
00:02:24,434 --> 00:02:28,776
Because the statistics are closed form, we
can precompute them once.

45
00:02:29,126 --> 00:02:33,293
But a table over signal, background and
depth would need a hundred and

46
00:02:33,293 --> 00:02:37,520
eight gigabytes, since depth must be
sampled at full timing resolution.

47
00:02:37,870 --> 00:02:43,048
Then we noticed: changing depth only
shifts the registered histogram in time.

48
00:02:43,398 --> 00:02:45,372
The count statistics do not move.

49
00:02:45,722 --> 00:02:48,495
So we drop depth from the table, and a
hundred and

50
00:02:48,495 --> 00:02:51,434
eight gigabytes becomes a hundred and five
megabytes.

51
00:02:51,934 --> 00:02:52,677
Does it work?

52
00:02:53,027 --> 00:02:57,006
Against the sequential gold standard, our
predicted count distribution

53
00:02:57,006 --> 00:03:01,212
matches in both centre and spread, where
earlier models miss the variance.

54
00:03:01,562 --> 00:03:04,452
The K L divergence is zero point zero one
three,

55
00:03:04,452 --> 00:03:07,402
thirty times better than the best previous
model.

56
00:03:07,752 --> 00:03:11,514
And on real hardware, a single photon
detector with an eighty five

57
00:03:11,514 --> 00:03:15,275
nanosecond dead time, our prediction lands
on the measured counts.

58
00:03:15,625 --> 00:03:19,596
This is hardware evidence, not just
agreement between simulators.

59
00:03:20,096 --> 00:03:21,129
Now the payoff.

60
00:03:21,479 --> 00:03:24,949
Generating one histogram cube of one
twenty eight by one twenty

61
00:03:24,949 --> 00:03:28,088
eight by one thousand twenty four takes
forty point eight

62
00:03:28,088 --> 00:03:31,998
seconds for the gold standard, and one
point zero one seconds for Mars.

63
00:03:32,348 --> 00:03:35,159
And here is the real point: the gold
standard

64
00:03:35,159 --> 00:03:38,095
is Monte Carlo, so it can never be
precomputed.

65
00:03:38,445 --> 00:03:41,127
Correct statistics, or practical speed.

66
00:03:41,477 --> 00:03:43,195
Until now, not both.

67
00:03:43,695 --> 00:03:45,286
And it matters downstream.

68
00:03:45,636 --> 00:03:47,974
We train the same network on data from
each

69
00:03:47,974 --> 00:03:51,127
simulator, then test all of them on gold
standard physics.

70
00:03:51,477 --> 00:03:54,486
The Poisson trained network collapses, to
seven

71
00:03:54,486 --> 00:03:58,199
decibels, with a depth error of one point
six five metres.

72
00:03:58,549 --> 00:04:01,080
Mars transfers almost perfectly.

73
00:04:01,430 --> 00:04:04,972
It has the smallest gap between its own
domain and the gold

74
00:04:04,972 --> 00:04:09,174
standard, and the best absolute result on
both reflectivity and depth.

75
00:04:09,674 --> 00:04:10,858
To summarize.

76
00:04:11,208 --> 00:04:14,389
Thirty times closer to the true photon
count law.

77
00:04:14,739 --> 00:04:17,131
A thousand times smaller lookup table.

78
00:04:17,481 --> 00:04:19,861
Forty times faster than the gold standard.

79
00:04:20,211 --> 00:04:23,462
And seventeen decibels better downstream
reconstruction.

80
00:04:23,962 --> 00:04:25,390
A few honest limits.

81
00:04:25,740 --> 00:04:28,087
We assume a non paralyzable detector,

82
00:04:28,087 --> 00:04:31,638
negligible after pulsing, and a single
return per pixel.

83
00:04:31,988 --> 00:04:35,227
Our prototype runs in num pie on a C P U.

84
00:04:35,577 --> 00:04:41,475
A G P U version, a compact table, and
paralyzable detectors are natural next
steps.

85
00:04:41,975 --> 00:04:46,004
The paper, the code, and the project page
are all linked below.

86
00:04:46,354 --> 00:04:47,480
Thank you for watching.

